---
import SaidDocsLayout from "../../../layouts/SaidDocsLayout.astro";
import SaidShot from "../../../components/SaidShot.astro";

const jsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "The keyboard is the desk",
  description:
    "Every Said and Done. flow without the mouse: one rule, a handful of keys, no modes.",
};
---

<SaidDocsLayout
  title="The keyboard is the desk — Said and Done. guides"
  description="Every Said and Done. flow without the mouse: moving between pages, opening them, filing them, finding them."
  section="guides"
  kicker="Guide 02"
  heading="The keyboard is the desk"
  dek="One rule, a handful of keys, no modes."
  slug="the-keyboard"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <h2 id="one-rule">The one rule</h2>
  <p>
    Said and Done. has one keyboard law and it never bends: <strong
      >a key goes to the topmost screen, and nowhere else</strong
    >. If the screen on top doesn't claim it, the key is dropped — it never
    falls through to the record underneath, never triggers something you can't
    see. There are no global shortcuts. Not for quit, not for search, not for
    anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    This sounds strict until you feel it. Every keypress is predictable from
    what's in front of your eyes, the help screen only ever shows keys that work
    right now, and a focused text field simply eats the keys — no modes to fall
    out of, no wrong-mode disasters to recover from.
  </p>

  <h2 id="moving">Moving around</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>j k</code> or <code>arrows</code> — walk the record, page by page.
    </li>
    <li><code>enter</code> or <code>o</code> — open the picked page.</li>
    <li><code>esc</code> — close the topmost screen, always.</li>
    <li>
      <code>g g</code> and <code>shift + g</code> — first page, last page.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/board-general.png"
    alt="The record with the cursor picked on a page, the strip below naming the next keys"
    caption="The strip below the record names the next keys for the picked page."
  />

  <h2 id="making">Making and moving</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>n</code> — a new page.</li>
    <li><code>e</code> — edit the picked page, title focused.</li>
    <li>
      <code>shift + h/l</code> — carry the picked page one status back or forward:
      backlog, to do, in progress, done.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>r</code> — resolve: done, dropped, or not relevant. The record keeps which.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>enter</code> opens the page's case file — its body, references, thread,
    and history on one sheet — and <code>esc</code> puts you back on the record exactly
    where you were.
  </p>

  <h2 id="finding">Finding</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <code>/</code> — the finder, every notebook answering; <code>?</code>
      keeps it to this notebook.
    </li>
    <li><code>p</code> — switch notebook; <code>b</code> — switch chapter.</li>
    <li><code>space h</code> — the key help, honest by construction.</li>
  </ul>
  <SaidShot
    src="/said-and-done/shots/finder.png"
    alt="The finder open over the record, exact and meaning hits together"
    caption="The finder rides over the record — exact and meaning hits together."
  />

  <p>
    If a key is shown on screen, it works. If it isn't, it doesn't. You can
    trust your hands to learn the desk in a day.
  </p>
</SaidDocsLayout>
